WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.100
- Time's almost up on this hour.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.770
But first, let's share one
last story I'm obsessed with.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:05.784
This is what I'm liking.

00:00:05.784 --> 00:00:08.820
(upbeat music)

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.080
All of us can only hope

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.780
that we can continue to follow
our dreams in retirement.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.120
A former Montana school
teacher's doing just that

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.710
in the most incredible way.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.020
She turned a van into a mobile bookstore

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.180
and she road trips across the country

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.190
sharing her love of reading with strangers

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.410
and making endless
friends along her journey.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:26.700
We have her on the line right now,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.470
dialed in from her traveling bookstore,

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.270
parked in Eureka, Montana.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.257
Everybody say hello to
Rita watching on KXLY 4.

00:00:35.144 --> 00:00:35.977
(audience cheering)

00:00:35.977 --> 00:00:36.810
Hi Rita.

00:00:39.407 --> 00:00:41.280
Hello, so this is really unique.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.440
So, what made you wanna do this?

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.130
- Well, I live in a really
small town in Montana,

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:51.180
1500 people, and I realized if
I wanted to open a bookstore

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.310
that it couldn't be a
brick and mortar bookstore

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:54.750
or I'd go broke.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.450
So, brainstormed and came up with the idea

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.340
of a traveling bookstore.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.340
I can take it where
there's people, festivals,

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.140
farmers markets, cities, and
set up and I have customers.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.850
And when I'm not on the road

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:12.510
then it just stays parked in my driveway.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.060
So I've been doing it for
about eight years now,

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.670
and I've traveled all across the country.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.770
I've been to the east coast three times,

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.140
down the west coast a bunch of times.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.950
Just came back from a
trip in the mountains

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:26.940
and Great Plains.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.323
So it's, yeah, it's a
wonderful experience.

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:35.323
I carry about six to 700 volumes
of books in the bookstore

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.830
and then I have some storage in the back

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.170
for extra books for when I'm on the road

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.790
for a long period of time.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:42.660
- You've gotta figure it out.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:43.493
That's rent free.

00:01:43.493 --> 00:01:44.326
That's awesome.

00:01:44.326 --> 00:01:45.570
That is, you've gotta figure it out.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.250
I would love, I love bookstores

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.950
and I love like small
boutique kind of things too

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.330
'cause you never know
what you'll find in them.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.760
So, I would adore this if I ran into you.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.020
I'm going to find you.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.720
But you say this isn't about
making money though, right?

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:58.553
- Right.

00:01:58.553 --> 00:02:01.200
I do make enough money to cover gas

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.050
and tires and insurance and fees,

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.240
things like that when I am on the road.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.600
So I say that the
bookstore pays for itself

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.310
and I pay for myself.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.579
So, that's the way it works.

00:02:14.579 --> 00:02:18.420
For the kids, I really feel
like books should be affordable.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.640
So I make all the kids books $1

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.431
whether they're picture books or.

00:02:22.431 --> 00:02:23.264
- [Kelly] Oh wow.

00:02:23.264 --> 00:02:24.240
- Chapter books.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.340
And that way kids can afford it.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.050
If they come to a farmer's market

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.080
and they wanna buy other
things, they can afford a book.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.043
- That's so cool.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.450
My kids would adore it as well.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:37.860
Thank you so much, Rita.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.000
This is really cool what you're doing.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.822
I'm liking your post right now, all right.

00:02:40.822 --> 00:02:42.960
(audience cheering)

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:43.800
And that's not all.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.679
Our season-long partner
Pilot Pen, Makers of G2,

00:02:45.679 --> 00:02:47.970
the go-to pen of The Kelly Clarkson Show

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:51.360
want to honor truly unstoppable
people just like you, ma'am.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.520
So they're gonna be writing
you a check for $1,000.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:54.450
How's that sound?

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:58.108
(audience cheering)

00:02:58.108 --> 00:03:00.023
- [Rita] Thank you so much.

00:03:00.023 --> 00:03:02.160
- No, thank you so much, Rita.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.020
And thank you to all our guests this hour,

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.420
George and Mayan Lopez, Allison Sweeney,

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:07.920
Maddy, Zach, and Jake Miller.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.820
Y'all have a great day and if
it's not, try and change it.

